      "file": "items/thm-normal-surface-unknot-algorithm-terminates-and-is-correct.md",
      "item_id": "thm-normal-surface-unknot-algorithm-terminates-and-is-correct",
      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** Countable choice, a valid one-component oriented knot code with $n$ crossings, and either search mode.\n\n[A1] $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is inherited through the smooth isotopy-extension and exterior-model interfaces.\n\n[F1] [[thm-haken-jaco-tollefson-unknot-recognition-procedure-properties]]: both finite search modes terminate on every finite code.\n\n[F2] [[thm-haken-finite-box-search-correctness]]: the Haken search accepts exactly when its triangulated exterior contains an essential disk, conditional on the Haken existence theorem.\n\n[F3] [[thm-jaco-tollefson-vertex-search-correctness]]: the JT search accepts exactly when its triangulated exterior contains an essential disk, conditional on the JT vertex-disk theorem.\n\n[F4] [[lem-meridian-disk-criterion-for-unknot-exterior]]: the represented smooth knot is unknotted exactly when its compatible PL exterior contains such a disk; the supplier proves the finite relative rounding, peripheral annulus and smooth ambient-isotopy implications.\n\n[F5] [[thm-smooth-knot-has-compatible-pl-exterior-model]]: the code's exterior has a good triangulation with $t\\le C(n+1)$ and the original meridian marked.\n\n[F6] [[lem-normal-coordinate-matching-equations-and-finite-bounds]]: Haken vectors have coordinates at most $t2^{7t+2}$ and primitive vertex vectors at most $2^{7t-1}$.\n\n\n\n1.1 The given countable-choice hypothesis [A1] supplies the choice assumption in [F5]. That item constructs a finite good exterior triangulation with $t\\le C(n+1)$ tetrahedra and a marked original meridian; the zero-crossing case uses its fixed product model. By [F1], validation and either search mode terminate. [given, A1, F1, F5, construct]\n\n1.2 For Haken mode, the box has $(t2^{7t+2}+1)^{7t}=2^{O(t^2)}$ vectors. Testing fundamentalness checks at most the same number of coordinatewise decompositions per vector, so the squared count remains $2^{O(t^2)}$; all tests on each compressed vector take polynomial time. Store only the current vector, one decomposition vector, counters and the matching matrix, using $O(t^2\\log(t+2))$ bits. [F1, F6, algebra]\n\n1.3 For JT mode, the support enumeration has at most $2^{7t}$ subsets. Each exact rank/kernel computation and candidate test takes polynomial time in $t$ and the $O(t)$-bit coordinates from [F6], giving $2^{O(t)}$ time. A single active matrix, candidate, and support counter use $O(t^2\\log(t+2))$ bits; no list of prior rays is stored because each ray is enumerated at its exact zero support. [F1, F6, algebra]\n\n2.1 In Haken mode, [F2] identifies acceptance exactly with existence of an essential disk in that exterior; by [F4], this is equivalent to the smooth knot being the unknot. In JT mode, the same conclusion follows from [F3] and [F4]. Thus each terminating branch returns the correct decision, conditional on the exact Haken and JT existence supplier proofs recorded in those items. [F2, F3, F4, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Substituting $t\\le C(n+1)$ in steps 1.2–3.2 yields the stated time and space bounds; the fixed $n=0$ branch is constant size. The statements in [F2]–[F4] still depend on the two exact unresolved normal-disk existence proof obligations identified in their contracts, so this theorem is escalated rather than ready. [F2, F3, F4, F5, step 1.2, step 1.3, algebra] ∎"
    },
    {
      "file": "items/thm-unknot-recognition-has-np-certificate.md",
      "item_id": "thm-unknot-recognition-has-np-certificate",
      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** Countable choice, a valid finite one-component knot code $D$, and the certificate format in the statement.\n\n[A1] $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is inherited through the smooth isotopy-extension and code-to-exterior interfaces.\n\n[F1] [[thm-smooth-knot-has-compatible-pl-exterior-model]]: $D$ has a smooth representative in its diagram type and a deterministic good exterior triangulation of size $t\\le C(n+1)$ with a marked original meridian.\n\n[F2] [[lem-normal-vertex-disk-certificate-verification]]: an accepted certificate gives a properly embedded disk with non-null-homotopic boundary, and verification is polynomial in its encoded length.\n\n[F3] [[thm-jaco-tollefson-normal-essential-disk-vertex-existence]]: if an essential disk exists, one is represented by a primitive normal vector on an extreme ray. Its proof is not supplied in the current item.\n\n[F4] [[lem-meridian-disk-criterion-for-unknot-exterior]]: unknotting is equivalent to an essential disk in the compatible PL exterior, by finite relative rounding and the smooth disk-boundary isotopy.\n\n[F5] [[lem-normal-coordinate-matching-equations-and-finite-bounds]]: each primitive vertex coordinate is at most $2^{7t-1}$.\n\n\n\n1.1 The given countable-choice hypothesis [A1] is the choice assumption used by [F1]'s exterior model. Reconstruct $T_D$ and the marked path using its fixed code order and local templates. The verifier does not accept a triangulation supplied by the certificate, so the certificate cannot substitute an unrelated manifold. [given, A1, F1, construct]\n\n2.1 If the verifier accepts, [F2] gives an essential disk in the exterior of the represented smooth knot. By [F4], the knot is the unknot. The relative rounding and disk-boundary isotopy in [F4] apply to exactly this compatible exterior model. [F2, F4, step 1.1, construct]\n\n2.2 Conversely, suppose the knot is the unknot. By [F4], its exterior contains an essential disk. By [F3], there is such a disk represented by a primitive vertex vector $v$. By [F5], each coordinate is at most $2^{7t-1}$ and therefore has $O(t)$ bits; the full vector has $O(t^2)$ bits. The active matching and zero-coordinate rows at this extreme ray have rank $7t-1$, so choose $7t-1$ independent rows and a nonzero square minor. Such a minor exists by the rank definition and has polynomial encoded length. The resulting certificate is accepted by the polynomial verifier [F2]. This completeness implication depends on the unresolved JT proof in [F3] ; the disk criterion [F4] supplies both required conversion directions. [F2, F3, F4, F5, step 1.1, construct, algebra]\n\n3.1 Since $t=O(n+1)$ by [F1], the vector, row indices and determinant witness all have polynomial bit length in $n$. The verifier reconstructs $T_D$ in polynomial time and checks matching equations, gcd, rank, boundary, Euler characteristic and meridian parity through compressed arithmetic as in [F2]; it never lists the exponentially many normal disks. [F1, F2, step 2.2, algebra] ∎"
